---@field project_id string Project ID of the project you're trying to access. To find the ID of the project, make a call to /api/projects/.
---@field task_id string
---@field id string
---@field before? number With tail_bytes, end the window before this entry index, so a client can page back through older turns: pass the X-Log-Offset of the oldest page it holds.
---@field start? number With tail_bytes, return every entry from this index to the end, with no preamble, so a client that holds the entries before it can catch up after a disconnect: pass the X-Log-Count of its last read. When those entries are too large, the response holds the latest turns as tail_bytes alone returns them, and X-Log-Offset is greater than start.
---@field tail_bytes? number Return only the latest turns of the log: the shortest suffix that starts at a user turn and holds at least this many bytes. The X-Log-Offset and X-Log-Count headers give the index of the first tail entry and the size of the full log. The slice starts with X-Log-Preamble earlier entries that still set run state, such as the run start, usage, mode, commands and open permission requests. X-Log-Turns-Before counts the user turns before the slice, so a client numbers turns the way the full log does.
---@field view? posthog.task_runs.GetTasksRunsLogRequestView|string 'full' returns every entry as stored. 'thread' drops the duplicate tool response copy in _meta.claudeCode.toolResponse and keeps only its decisionReason and message, for clients that render the conversation. It applies to the full log and to every slice. * `full` - full * `thread` - thread

---@alias posthog.task_runs.GetTasksRunsLogRequestView "full"|"thread"

